$ gdb -q -batch -ex 'help break' | head -n 3
break, brea, bre, br, b
Set breakpoint at specified location.
break [PROBE_MODIFIER] [LOCATION] [thread THREADNUM]
$ gdb -q -batch -ex 'help watch' | head -n 3
Set a watchpoint for EXPRESSION.
Usage: watch [-location] EXPRESSION

$ gdb -q -batch -ex 'help backtrace' | head -n 3
backtrace, where, bt
Print backtrace of all stack frames, or innermost COUNT frames.
Usage: backtrace [OPTION]... [QUALIFIER]... [COUNT | -COUNT]
$ gdb -q -batch -ex 'help frame' | head -n 3
frame, f
Select and print a stack frame.
With no argument, print the selected stack frame.  (See also "info frame").
$ gdb -q -batch -ex 'help info locals' | head -n 3
All local variables of current stack frame or those matching REGEXPs.
Usage: info locals [-q] [-t TYPEREGEXP] [NAMEREGEXP]
Prints the local variables of the current stack frame.
$ gdb -q -batch -ex 'help next' | head -n 3
next, n
Step program, proceeding through subroutine calls.
Usage: next [N]
$ gdb -q -batch -ex 'help step' | head -n 3
step, s
Step program until it reaches a different source line.
Usage: step [N]
$ gdb -q -batch -ex 'help finish' | head -n 3
finish, fin
Execute until selected stack frame returns.
Usage: finish
$ gdb -q -batch -ex 'help print' | head -n 3
print, inspect, p
Print value of expression EXP.
Usage: print [[OPTION]... --] [/FMT] [EXP]
$ gdb -q -batch -ex 'help continue' | head -n 3
continue, fg, c
Continue program being debugged, after signal or breakpoint.
Usage: continue [N]
$ lldb -b -o 'help breakpoint set' | sed -n '2,3p'
Sets a breakpoint or set of breakpoints in the executable.

$ lldb -b -o 'help watchpoint set variable' | sed -n '2,3p'
Set a watchpoint on a variable. Use the '-w' option to specify the type of
watchpoint and the '-s' option to specify the byte size to watch for. If no
$ lldb -b -o 'help frame variable' | sed -n '2,3p'
Show variables for the current stack frame. Defaults to all arguments and local
variables in scope. Names of argument, local, file static and file global
$ lldb -b -o 'help thread backtrace' | sed -n '2,3p'
Show thread call stacks.  Defaults to the current thread, thread indexes can be
specified as arguments.
